For each criterion, inclusion and exclusion alike, in the order above, give exactly one assessment, even when another criterion already decides, in this order:
1. "evidence_quote": the exact words of the title or abstract the assessment rests on (in their original language); leave it empty when the reference says nothing about the criterion. Never quote words that are not in the reference.
2. "finding": one short sentence saying what the reference shows about the criterion. For an exclusion criterion, say whether the reason for exclusion is true of the reference.
3. "status", which follows from the finding:
   - for an inclusion criterion: "met" when the reference satisfies it; "not_met" when it clearly does not;
   - for an exclusion criterion: "met" when the reason for exclusion is true of the reference, so that it should be excluded for this reason; "not_met" when the reason clearly does not apply. Read the criterion as written, negative wording included: an exclusion criterion such as "Did not study X" is "met" when the reference does not study X, and "not_met" when it does;
   - "cannot_tell" when the reference says nothing clear about the criterion (missing or vague abstract).

Criteria on the publication itself (date, language, type of publication) are judged on the reference as published, not on when its data were collected. The year given may be the year of the printed issue, which can come a year or more after the first online publication: when that year lies just outside a date limit of a criterion, the criterion is "cannot_tell", not "not_met". Compare dates exactly: a year within the limits of a date criterion, limits included, satisfies it.

Then give, in agreement with your assessments:
- "rationale": one or two sentences naming the decisive criteria by their codes, written in the language identified by this ISO 639-1 code: {{ language }}. A reference is excluded only for an inclusion criterion that is "not_met" or an exclusion criterion that is "met".
- "decision": "include", "exclude" or "uncertain". Exclude only when at least one inclusion criterion is "not_met" or one exclusion criterion is "met". If no criterion is failed in this way and some cannot be told, the decision is "uncertain".
- "inclusion_probability": the probability, from 0 to 1, that a careful reviewer applying these criteria would keep this reference for the full-text stage. Below 0.5 only when at least one criterion is failed as above: a reference that fails no criterion cannot be unlikely to be kept.
- "decisive_criteria": the codes of the criteria that decide.

References may be written in any language: apply the criteria to their content.
